WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.780
Every pipeline answer ends with one question.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.815
How is it scheduled, retried, and backfilled?

3
00:00:06.835 --> 00:00:09.840
Three ideas answer it: what an orchestrator does,

4
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.215
data intervals, and idempotent tasks.

5
00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:15.700
First: what is an orchestrator for?

6
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.325
It starts tasks on time.

7
00:00:17.345 --> 00:00:18.550
It runs them in order.

8
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.025
It retries failures.

9
00:00:20.045 --> 00:00:22.035
And it shows you what happened.

10
00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:24.135
It should not do the heavy work itself.

11
00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:27.485
It tells Spark or the warehouse to do it.

12
00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:30.595
Second: the idea people get wrong in Airflow.

13
00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:32.570
A daily run does not mean today.

14
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.005
It covers one full day of data.

15
00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:37.805
So the run for Monday starts after Monday ends.

16
00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:39.890
The data is complete then.

17
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.990
Use the interval dates in your queries.

18
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.840
Never use the clock time of now.

19
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.075
Third: any run can run again.

20
00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:50.175
A retry or a backfill runs the same task twice.

21
00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:52.335
The result must be the same.

22
00:00:52.355 --> 00:00:54.172
An append adds the rows again.

23
00:00:54.192 --> 00:00:55.995
Now you have duplicates.

24
00:00:56.015 --> 00:00:58.395
So overwrite the partition of that interval.

25
00:00:58.415 --> 00:01:00.920
Or use a merge on a key.

26
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.805
Last: Airflow, Prefect, or another tool?

27
00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:08.190
Airflow is the standard, with a schedule and a graph you define first.

28
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:13.740
Prefect runs normal Python functions, and the flow can change while it runs.

29
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.925
Quick recap of orchestration.

30
00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:17.413
The orchestrator directs.

31
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:19.050
A run covers an interval.

32
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.510
And every task can run twice.

33
00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.630
Now start the first lesson below.
